════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
M1 — the fold-rule A/B, in-run, runcf, 2026-09-07
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

THE QUESTION. runce reported P9 repack amplification at 1.75x against
runcd's 0.94x, and `runce-fold-fix-2026-09-07.log` attributed it to the
strict supersede rule shipped in aaffe335, naming the mechanism: "an
input the roll-up does not fully hold stays named, so a later fold takes
it as an input again and re-uploads its contents". That was a CROSS-RUN,
CROSS-ORDER comparison, and the same log said so and named the
experiment that would settle it. This is that experiment.

DESIGN. One cluster (trove 145 `runcf`, CP + 2 workers, ALL SPOT
i4i.xlarge, us-west-1). Two images built from the SAME commit
(dfcd18f0), differing ONLY in the strict supersede rule — verified by
CONTENT, not by tag: the marker string "the fold keeps" is present in
`ab-strict` (1) and absent in `ab-oldfold` (0), checked inside the
running images. Six runs, ABBA+BA ordering (strict, oldfold, oldfold,
strict, oldfold, strict) so ordinal position cannot pose as the effect.
Each run got its OWN FlintRepo and prefix, so bytes are exactly
attributable, and each ran the identical legs P0+P9+P2.

NON-VACUITY, and this is what three previous local attempts could not
achieve: the rule FIRED in every strict run and in no oldfold run.

  run  arm      P9 wall  P9 median  P2 rate    rule fired  resident bytes  objects
   1   strict    34 s     0.57 s    14.7/s         1       1,143,675,655    2958
   2   oldfold   35 s     0.56 s    16.1/s         0       1,143,875,221    3245
   3   oldfold   34 s     0.56 s    16.2/s         0       1,144,005,403    3270
   4   strict    35 s     0.58 s    14.0/s         1       1,143,659,292    2832
   5   oldfold   41 s     0.59 s    14.7/s         0       1,143,687,894    2980
   6   strict    34 s     0.55 s    15.2/s         1       1,143,746,462    3050

─────────────────────────── THE RESULT ─────────────────────────────────

THE PRE-REGISTERED FALSIFIER FIRES. It said: "the two arms are within
0.15x of each other on P9 ⇒ the cross-run difference was repository
order, the mechanism named is wrong, and the byte problem is somewhere
else entirely — stop and re-diagnose before touching fold.rs."

  resident bytes  strict mean 1,143,693,803   oldfold mean 1,143,856,173
                  difference 162,370 bytes = 0.014%

That is not 0.15x. It is not 0.01x. The two arms are the same to within
a rounding error on a 1.14 GB repository.

**So the strict supersede rule does NOT explain runce's 0.94x → 1.75x.
The attribution in `runce-fold-fix-2026-09-07.log` is WRONG, and that
file has been corrected to say so.** The remaining explanation is the
one that log already flagged as a confound: P9 ran FIRST on runce and
after P1/P4 on runcd, against a different repository state.

AND THE THIRD PAIR CHANGED THE ANSWER. After four runs the P2 rate
looked like a real cost — strict 14.7/14.0 against oldfold 16.1/16.2,
consistent and not ordinal. Two more runs dissolved it:

  P2 rate   strict  14.0  14.7  15.2   (mean 14.63)
            oldfold 14.7  16.1  16.2   (mean 15.67)

The ranges OVERLAP: oldfold's slowest run is identical to strict's
fastest-but-one. With n=3 per arm there is no established throughput
difference, and the 11% that four runs suggested was noise wearing a
pattern. Objects overlap the same way (strict 2832-3050, oldfold
2980-3270). P9 wall and median are a dead heat throughout.

CONCLUSION: on this workload the strict supersede rule has NO
MEASURABLE COST — not in bytes, not in throughput, not in latency —
while demonstrably firing. The correctness it buys is free here.

────────────────────── WHAT THIS COST TO LEARN ─────────────────────────

Two rig defects, both found by this drill.

1. **P9 PASSED WHILE MEASURING NOTHING.** The first attempt ran
   `LEGS='P9 P2'` without P0 — and P0 is the leg that installs
   `/work/seqpush.sh` into the agent pod. Every push failed, no per-push
   log came back, and P9's guard computed `nbad` with awk over a missing
   file, got zero, and reported "48 pushes told ok in 0 s; per push
   median = none" as a PASS. Fixed: the count of SUCCESSFUL rows is now
   the guard and an absent log is INCONCLUSIVE with the cause named.

2. **CLOUDWATCH CANNOT RESOLVE THIS LEG.** S3 request metrics are summed
   per MINUTE; P9 runs for 34 seconds and P2 begins immediately after.
   Padded windows swallowed the neighbouring leg, and the first byte
   numbers pulled that way were incoherent — strict held both the lowest
   (1144 MB) and the highest (1953 MB) of four runs. Per-prefix resident
   bytes are the right instrument for a leg this short, and they are
   exact. Recorded so the next drill does not repeat it.

Teardown: instances, volumes, spot requests, elastic IPs, trove SGs,
snapshots, projects and orphans all zero; bucket deleted on the first
attempt (versioning deliberately OFF this time — it is what forced two
version-purge passes on runce); IAM user gone; keyfile shredded.
